# Line endings, as repo policy rather than per-user config.
#
# `core.autocrlf` already does the "LF in the repo, native in the working
# tree" dance, and it is set to true on the Windows machine here. The
# problem with relying on it is that it is LOCAL CONFIG: it applies to
# whoever happens to have set it, and not to a fresh clone, a colleague,
# or CI. These attributes are checked in, so they apply to everyone.
#
# `text=auto` is the checked-in equivalent of autocrlf: normalise to LF on
# commit, convert to the platform's native ending on checkout. Adding it
# here changed no file content — the index was already 100% LF — so it
# costs nothing and removes the dependence on a local setting.
#
# The eol= overrides below are the part that matters, because for these
# two extensions "native to the platform" is the wrong answer:
#
#   *.sh   Must stay LF even on Windows. Git Bash tolerates a CRLF script,
#          which is why scripts/flash-dfu.sh sat in the working tree with
#          CRLF and ran fine for months. Real Linux bash does not, and on
#          this machine WSL is one command away reading the same files
#          through /mnt/c. Measured:
#
#              wsl bash -n scripts/flash-dfu.sh
#              syntax error near unexpected token `$'do\r''
#
#          Note this never affected a Linux CLONE: the index is LF, so a
#          checkout on Debian has always been correct. The breakage is
#          specific to running the WINDOWS working tree from WSL.
#
#   *.cmd  Batch is happier with CRLF, and unlike the above it is only
#          ever run by cmd.exe, so there is no cross-platform case.
* text=auto
*.sh text eol=lf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
